     These functions are used to compose mbuf chains from various data types.
     The _m_b_c_h_a_i_n structure is used as a working context and should be initial‐
     ized with a call to either mmbb__iinniitt() or mmbb__iinniittmm().  It has the following
     fields:

     _m_b___t_o_p    (_s_t_r_u_c_t _m_b_u_f _*) A pointer to the top of constructed mbuf chain.

     _m_b___c_u_r    (_s_t_r_u_c_t _m_b_u_f _*) A pointer to the currently filled mbuf.

     _m_b___m_l_e_f_t  (_i_n_t) Number of bytes left in the current mbuf.

     _m_b___c_o_u_n_t  (_i_n_t) Total number of bytes placed in the mbuf chain.

     _m_b___c_o_p_y   (_m_b___c_o_p_y___t _*) User-defined function to perform a copy into
               mbuf; useful if any unusual data conversion is necessary.

     _m_b___u_d_a_t_a  (_v_o_i_d _*) User-supplied data which can be used in the _m_b___c_o_p_y
               function.

     mmbb__ddoonnee() function disposes an mbuf chain pointed to by _m_b_p_-_>_m_b___t_o_p field
     and sets the field to NULL.

     mmbb__ddeettaacchh() function returns the value of _m_b_p_-_>_m_b___t_o_p field and sets its
     value to NULL.

     mmbb__ffiixxhhddrr() recalculates the length of an mbuf chain and updates the
     _m___p_k_t_h_d_r_._l_e_n field of the first mbuf in the chain.  It returns the calcu‐
     lated length.

     mmbb__rreesseerrvvee() ensures that the object of the length specified by the _s_i_z_e
     argument will fit in the current mbuf (mbuf allocation is performed if
     necessary), and advances all pointers as if the real data was placed.
     Returned value will point to the beginning of the reserved space.  Note
     that the size of the object should not exceed MLEN bytes.

     All mmbb__ppuutt__**() functions perform an actual copy of the data into mbuf
     chain.  Functions which have llee or bbee suffixes will perform conversion to
     the little- or big-endian data formats.

     mmbb__ppuutt__mmeemm() function copies _s_i_z_e bytes of data specified by the _s_o_u_r_c_e
     argument to an mbuf chain.  The _t_y_p_e argument specifies the method used
     to perform a copy, and can be one of the following:

     MB_MSYSTEM  Use bbccooppyy() function.

     MB_MUSER    Use copyin(9) function.

     MB_MINLINE  Use an “inline” loop which does not call any function.

     MB_MZERO    Do not copy any data, but just fill the destination with zero
                 bytes.

     MB_MCUSTOM  Call function specified by the _m_b_p_-_>_m_b___c_o_p_y field.

